        'In the first phase of the CRIBS programme (2024-2026), Jigsaw and research partners evaluated UNICEF’s climate-resilient infrastructure interventions in Kano and Jigawa, alongside implementation-based research in Lagos examining the effects of specific low-cost infrastructure retrofits. The second phase has expanded the CRIBS evaluation to four states: Enugu, Gombe, Katsina, and Jigawa, while the Lagos research continues to examine the effects and feasibility of low-cost adaptations outside the UNICEF programme. Across both strands of work, the research uses a multi-phase mixed-methods design to assess the effectiveness, feasibility and cost-effectiveness of infrastructure adaptations in strengthening climate resilience in primary education and health facilities. It examines exposure and vulnerability to multiple climate hazards, including flooding, extreme heat, drought and storms. Methods include rapid evidence review, context analysis, climate data analysis, hazard exposure and vulnerability assessments, engineering assessments and infrastructure surveys, environmental quality monitoring using sensors, assessments of staff cognitive performance and wellbeing, and qualitative research on perceptions and experiences. The research covers 66 facilities across six states, with different components designed to assess changes in environmental conditions and climate vulnerability before and after intervention, while also examining the feasibility, cost-effectiveness and potential for scale of different approaches to climate-resilient infrastructure.',
      pathwaysToUptake:
        'Phase one evidence on climate hazards, facility vulnerabilities and environmental quality has been shared with government officials, UNICEF and FCDO. Findings have informed the selection, implementation and monitoring of low-cost adaptations, as well as the overall design of programme implementation used in the second phase of CRIBS.Further evidence generated from the research is intended to inform future climate-resilient infrastructure programming across education and health facilities in Nigeria and other low- and middle-income contexts.'
